      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are correct that at high pH, the amino groups remain unprotonated, enhancing their reactivity in the Maillard reaction. In acidic environments (high pH), protons (H+) react with amino groups (-NH2) to form protonated amino groups (-NH3+), making them less reactive and slowing down the initiation of the Maillard reaction. Under acidic conditions, the protonated amino groups are less accessible for conjugations with reducing sugars, thereby reducing browning rates. Conversely, at lower pH, the unprotonated amino groups are more reactive, and the Maillard reaction proceeds faster.",
